proofs.proof_tex:
The PPML coefficient is feasible for the target gradient. Indeed, using the definitions of \(S\) and \(h_{\mathrm{CM}}\), and commutativity of \(M\) and \(P_U\),
\[
J^{\prime}h_{\mathrm{CM}}
=\begin{pmatrix}X^{\prime}MP_UXS^{-1}c_\beta\\c_D\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}c_\beta\\c_D\end{pmatrix}
=c.                                                      \tag{1}
\]
Every other solution of \(J^{\prime}h=c\) is \(h_{\mathrm{CM}}+R\gamma\). Since \(K=R^{\prime}\Omega R\succ0\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
(h_{\mathrm{CM}}+R\gamma)^{\prime}\Omega(h_{\mathrm{CM}}+R\gamma)
&=h_{\mathrm{CM}}^{\prime}\Omega h_{\mathrm{CM}}
-b_{\mathrm{cyc}}^{\prime}K^{-1}b_{\mathrm{cyc}}\\
&\quad+
(\gamma+K^{-1}b_{\mathrm{cyc}})^{\prime}
K(\gamma+K^{-1}b_{\mathrm{cyc}}).
\end{aligned}                                           \tag{2}
\]
Thus the unique minimum is attained by
\[
h_{\mathrm{CM}}-RK^{-1}b_{\mathrm{cyc}}.                \tag{3}
\]
Lagrange minimization of \(h^{\prime}\Omega h\) subject to \(J^{\prime}h=c\) gives \(\Omega^{-1}J(J^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}J)^{-1}c=h^{\star}\). Hence (3) equals \(h^{\star}\). For \(q=0\), the same conclusion holds with empty cycle matrices.

The consistency results in the PPML proof and continuity give, on events with probability tending to one,
\[
\widehat R\to_PR,\quad
\widehat\Omega\to_P\Omega,\quad
\widehat h_{\mathrm{CM}}\to_Ph_{\mathrm{CM}},\quad
\widehat b_{\mathrm{cyc}}\to_Pb_{\mathrm{cyc}},\quad
\widehat K\to_PK.                                       \tag{4}
\]
For a fundamental cycle \(e=(g,t)\), the population log contrast is zero because \(\mu_{gt}=a_gb_t\). Positivity of its four cell means permits Taylor expansion:
\[
\sqrt N\widehat\kappa
=R^{\prime}\sqrt N(\overline Y-m)+o_P(1).               \tag{5}
\]
Combining the PPML expansion with (4)--(5), and using \(\widehat\kappa=O_P(N^{-1/2})\), gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt N(\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{eff}}-\theta_{\mathrm{PTT}})
&=\{h_{\mathrm{CM}}-RK^{-1}b_{\mathrm{cyc}}\}^{\prime}
\sqrt N(\overline Y-m)+o_P(1)\\
&=h^{\star\prime}\sqrt N(\overline Y-m)+o_P(1).
\end{aligned}                                           \tag{6}
\]
The fallback on \(\mathcal E_N^c\) is irrelevant because \(\Pr(\mathcal E_N^c)\to0\). Moreover,
\[
h^{\star\prime}\sqrt N(\overline Y-m)
=N^{-1/2}\sum_{g\in C}\sum_{i=1}^{n_g}
\frac{h_g^{\star\prime}(Y_{gi}-m_g)}{p_g}+o_P(1),        \tag{7}
\]
where replacing \(n_g/N\) by \(p_g\) costs \(o_P(1)\). The summand is the stratified representation of \(\phi^{\star}\). The fixed-strata central limit theorem gives variance \(h^{\star\prime}\Omega h^{\star}=V^{\star}\), which the canonical-gradient theorem identifies as the lower bound. Thus the feasible estimator attains \(V^{\star}\) pointwise.
